Port 22
PermitRootLogin no
PubkeyAuthentication yes
AuthorizedKeysFile .ssh/authorized_keys
PasswordAuthentication no
AllowTcpForwarding yes
GatewayPorts no
X11Forwarding no
Subsystem sftp /usr/lib/ssh/sftp-server

# Issue #2150: OpenSSH >= 9.8 enables PerSourcePenalties by default, and this
# fleet's alpine base now ships 10.3p1. The feature blocks a SOURCE ADDRESS
# after its penalties accumulate past `min:15s` (authfail:5s, noauth:1s,
# crash:90s).
#
# That is correct on the public internet and actively harmful here, because a
# test fixture has exactly ONE source. Every emulator/host client reaches a
# published container port through the single docker gateway (172.17.0.1), so
# sshd cannot tell one journey lane from another: four routine harness auth
# failures — the ordinary rhythm of a run — put 20s of penalty on that address
# and sshd then refuses EVERY lane, including lanes that did nothing wrong.
#
# The failure is worse than a flake. A penalised lane presents as connection
# failures inside the product under test, which is indistinguishable from a
# real regression in SSH connect/lease/reattach — the exact area PocketShell's
# journeys exercise. It manufactures evidence pointing at a product bug that
# does not exist (#2150 cost two full emulator runs in #2111 before diagnosis;
# same class as the #1842 agents-pool collision).
#
# Rate-limiting by source is meaningless for a disposable single-source
# fixture, so turn it off rather than tune it. The compose healthcheck in
# tests/docker/docker-compose.yml asserts this is in effect, so a fixture that
# reverts to penalising can never report `healthy`.
PerSourcePenalties no
